{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S6 — Clear the parity bar for Bedrock before it becomes a first-class provider\n\n## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/llm/parity.py` defines rebar's non-inferiority bar — structured-output validity, per-criterion\nverdict agreement with zero decision-level flips on the gold set, recall and false-accept within 2pp, runtime\nerror rate, and a gold-coverage floor. It was required before the last LLM substrate swap (LangGraph →\npydantic-ai). Routing gate traffic through Bedrock is a substrate change of the same kind: a different API\nsurface (Converse), different capability flags, and a different structured-output path. S8 (eb6e) cuts the\nproduction review bot over to Bedrock; without a measured bar, that cutover and the epic's \"first-class\nprovider\" tier label are unearned assertions. This story produces the measurement S8 consumes.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd an opt-in, operator-run provider-parity harness modeled directly on the landed spot-eval precedent\n`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/fidelity_spot_eval.py` — same parity bar, same epoch-majority aggregation, same\ncommitted-results-plus-offline-recheck shape. That precedent is the positive reason for the choice: it is the\none pattern in this repo that has already run a paired, live, billable two-arm comparison through\n`parity.parity_report` and left reviewable evidence behind (`src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/fidelity_spot_eval_results.json`,\nre-scored offline by `tests/unit/test_fidelity_spot_eval.py::test_recorded_live_results_are_within_tolerance`).\nCopying a proven harness shape is cheaper and lower-risk than inventing a second one.\n\n**Correcting an earlier claim in this plan.** A previous draft said \"no new gate logic is written; wiring only\".\nThat is FALSE and nothing here relies on it. `parity_report` itself is pure and needs no change, but it has ZERO\nproduction callers — under `src/` the only references are its own definition and two internal reuses,\n`container_fidelity_report` and `prerequisite_fidelity_report` — and `run_eval` returns\n`{prompt, epochs, gate, passed, coverage, epoch_pass, junit}`, i.e. per-EPOCH pass/fail, not per-item\ndecision + gold label (`src/rebar/llm/evals/eval.py:622-649`). The corpus→`ItemRecord` adapter, the repeat\naggregation, the two-arm driver, the CLI and the results serializer are all NET-NEW and load-bearing, because\ncriterion (b) \"zero decision-level flips\" is scored entirely on that mapping.\n\n**Riskiest assumption, tested first.** The assumption most likely to sink the run is that the Bedrock\ninference-profile ids are invokable at all in THIS project's AWS account. Ticket 2932's operator correction\nrecords that every earlier Bedrock measurement was taken in a different account and region and assigns the\nre-measurement to this story; `infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf:26-28` records a real `AccessDeniedException` on\n`bedrock:InvokeModel`, and a plain on-demand id was refused with `ValidationException`. So **step 1 is a probe,\nnot the corpus**: one ~8-token `aws bedrock-runtime converse` call per candidate inference-profile id in account\n896586841071 / us-east-1 — the account of the terraform backend bucket `rebar-tfstate-896586841071`\n(`infra/terraform/versions.tf:14`) and the `aws_region` default `us-east-1`\n(`infra/terraform/variables.tf:1-5`). A refusal stops the run before a dollar of corpus budget is spent, and the\nrefusal itself is the recorded finding.\n\n**Exactly one variable between the arms.** Both arms run the SAME cases through the SAME\n`rebar.llm.evals.eval_solver.run_case` path; only the model string differs. The mechanism is\n`rebar.llm.config.gate_config(cfg)` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:93-101`): inside that scope `resolve_gate_config`\nreturns the arm's config, and every eval-solver arm resolves its `RunRequest.config` from `resolve_gate_config`\n(`src/rebar/llm/evals/eval_solver.py:103,155,229`). This is load-bearing rather than incidental, because\n`runner._effective_config` substitutes `req.config.model` OVER the runner's own base config\n(`src/rebar/llm/runner.py:209-212`) — passing a differently-configured runner alone would NOT flip the model.\nWrapping each arm in `gate_config` makes the request config and the runner agree, so provider is the only\nvariable and any criterion failure is attributable.\n\nThe gate's per-pass model ladder is deliberately NOT in play: `RunRequest` (`src/rebar/llm/runner.py:70-118`)\ncarries no model-class field, so a single-prompt eval case resolves `cfg.model` and nothing else. That is why\nthis harness — rather than a paired `rebar review-plan` run — is the right instrument: the split-brain risk\nrecorded earlier on this ticket (a bare `claude-*` ladder rung silently routing a \"Bedrock\" arm to direct\nAnthropic) cannot arise on this path.\n\n**Arms and pairing.** Same underlying model on both sides of each pair, named by class slot:\n\n- `standard`: v1 `claude-sonnet-4-6` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:73`) ↔ v2\n  `bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6` (`src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:38`).\n- `frontier`: v1 `claude-opus-4-8` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:67`) ↔ v2 the `us.`-prefixed inference profile for\n  the same opus model, using whichever id step 1 confirms invokable.\n\nBoth slots run because production uses both — frontier is the Pass-1 finder, standard is Pass-2 verify and\nPass-4 coach. Certifying only sonnet would leave the frontier path, which does most of the gate's work,\nunmeasured.\n\n**Corpus and the gold floor.** `src/rebar/llm/parity.py:88` sets `MIN_GOLD_ITEMS = 20` and `parity_report`\nFAILS below it; no single eval spec's gold set reaches 20 (largest: `plan-review-container.eval.yaml`, 12). So\nthe corpus POOLS gold-labelled dataset cases across the 21 dataset-bearing specs under\n`src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/`: 169 cases carry an `expect` in `{finding, fail, pass}`, mapping to the parity\ndecision vocabulary as `finding`/`fail` → `block` and `pass` → `advisory` — the same two-bucket mapping\n`fidelity_spot_eval._verdict_decision` already uses. The run uses a deterministic stratified sample of 40 of\nthose (up to two per spec, balanced finding/pass, ordered by spec id then case id) with a recorded corpus digest\nin the shape of `fidelity_spot_eval.prerequisite_corpus_digest`. 40 clears the floor of 20 with margin at a\nbounded budget, so **`min_gold` is NOT lowered** — `parity_report` is called with its default. The CI cost rail\n`REBAR_EVAL_MAX_CASES` is deliberately not used: it truncates `dataset[:12]` per spec\n(`src/rebar/llm/evals/eval.py:654-655`) and would decapitate the largest gold sources.\n\nWorked example of one corpus item, so the adapter's contract is unambiguous: dataset case\n`G3-R-uncovered-ac-rebar` in `plan-review-container.eval.yaml` carries `expect: finding`, so its gold `label` is\n`\"block\"`. Its three v2 epochs return findings / findings / no-findings, so `_verdict_decision` yields\n`block, block, advisory`, the majority is `block`, and the arm's record is\n`ItemRecord(valid=True, decision=\"block\", label=\"block\")` — a recall hit and not a flip. Had the v1 arm's\nmajority been `advisory` on the same item, `parity_report` would emit\n`1 decision-level flip(s) on the gold set (must be 0)` and `passed=False`.\n\n**Repeats.** `parity.ItemRecord`'s \"averaged over the N repeats\" is prose, not behaviour:\n`parallel_run_and_diff` (`src/rebar/llm/parity.py:286-302`) runs each item exactly once per runner with no\nrepeat loop and no averaging. So this harness does NOT use `parallel_run_and_diff`. It runs its own N=3 epoch\nloop per case per arm and reduces to a majority decision (ties broken toward the cautious `block`), exactly as\n`fidelity_spot_eval.relocation_spot_eval(epochs=…)` and `_majority_decision` already do, then feeds the averaged\npaired `ItemRecord` lists straight to `parity_report`.\n\n**Budget.** 40 cases × 2 arms × 3 epochs × 2 class slots = 480 case-runs. Case-runs are not uniformly one model\ncall: the criterion, novelty and code-review arms of `run_case` are single-prompt, while `completion-verifier`,\n`ticket-quality`, `spec-alignment` and `code-quality` take an agentic disposable-store path. Actual per-call\nmodel usage is therefore measured, not assumed, via `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` — the rig\n`.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml:83,110-112` already uses — and summarized into the recorded report.\n\n**Not a GitHub Actions job.** The harness is an operator-run module CLI, like `fidelity_spot_eval.main()`, which\nno workflow invokes. A CI job was the earlier plan and is rejected on grounded terms: the Bedrock grant in\n`infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf:57-72` is attached to the Gerrit **instance** role, and the only GitHub-OIDC role,\n`rebar-terraform-plan` (`infra/terraform/iam.tf:166-178`, assumed at\n`.github/workflows/terraform-drift.yml:34-39`), is `ReadOnlyAccess` — so a CI arm would first require a new\nBedrock-capable OIDC role, which is unfunded work outside this story. Auth is therefore the ambient AWS\ncredential chain (`src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:10,55-88`) with `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION=us-east-1` set\nexplicitly (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:569-571`), plus `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for the v1 arm. Region is set\nexplicitly because an unresolvable region otherwise surfaces as a bare boto3 `NoRegionError` from deep inside\nclient construction (`src/rebar/llm/bedrock_model.py:67-87`).\n\n**Decision-bearing.** If Bedrock fails any criterion, this ticket records the failure and the epic's\nfirst-class claim for Bedrock is revisited before the S8 cutover proceeds, rather than shipping a tier label the\nevidence does not support.\n\n**Out of scope — cited, not absorbed.** The sub-4096-token \"caching had NO effect\" warning is miscalibrated on\nBOTH providers (bug 7a79-a920-afe7-40cf); it must not be read as a Bedrock caching regression when this run\nemits it. `MODEL_LADDER`'s equal sonnet/opus context limits (1157) and the mis-located escalation START rung for\na non-Anthropic-family primary (48b3) affect ladder escalation, which this harness does not exercise.\n`infer_provider`'s `gemini` → `google_genai` mapping (5267) is unrelated to the `bedrock:` prefix.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/provider_parity.py` (new) — the corpus selector and digest, the two-arm `gate_config`\n  driver, the epoch-majority `ItemRecord` adapter, the `parity_report` call, the results serializer, and the\n  `main()` CLI including the step-1 invokability probe.\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/provider_parity_results.json` (new) — the committed recorded run, in the\n  `fidelity_spot_eval.record_results` shape plus each arm's resolved model id, the corpus digest, the epoch\n  count and the per-arm call/latency summary.\n- `tests/unit/test_provider_parity.py` (new) — the offline tests below, using `FakeRunner`.\n- This ticket's comments — the quoted `ParityReport` verdict and the probe result.\n\n## Testing\n\nOffline, in `make test`, no model and no credentials:\n\n- Happy path: two agreeing arms over a fixture corpus produce `passed=True` with zero decision flips.\n- Failure path: a candidate arm that flips one gold decision produces `passed=False` and the emitted\n  `gating_failures` names `decision-level flip(s) on the gold set`.\n- Edge: a corpus below the gold floor fails on the coverage guard alone, mirroring\n  `tests/unit/test_parity_bar.py::test_goldless_run_fails_coverage_guard`.\n- Edge: an arm whose runner raises records an `errored` `ItemRecord` and the comparison completes rather than\n  aborting.\n- Recheck: the committed `provider_parity_results.json` is re-scored against the same parity bar with no model\n  call, mirroring `test_recorded_live_results_are_within_tolerance`.\n\nLive, opt-in, operator-run, never in default CI:\n\n- Step 1, the probe: each candidate inference-profile id is invoked once and its outcome recorded, before any\n  corpus spend.\n- Step 2: the paired corpus run at N=3 per arm per class slot, with `REBAR_USAGE_LOG` used to assert the v2 arm\n  made ZERO calls resolving to a non-Bedrock provider. Extract per-call model only from log lines carrying BOTH\n  `model=` and `input_tokens=`; a whole-log grep for the model id over-counts, because verdict-JSON fields match\n  the same string.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The offline test module is green: `pytest tests/unit/test_provider_parity.py` passes, covering the\n      agreeing-arms, flipped-decision, below-gold-floor, errored-arm and recorded-results-recheck cases.\n- [ ] The harness cannot run a live model in default CI: `make test` passes with no AWS and no Anthropic\n      credentials present, and `grep -rn provider_parity .github/workflows/` returns no match.\n- [ ] `parity_report` is called with its default `min_gold` — the corpus supplies at least 20 gold items and\n      the floor is never lowered; verified by the offline recheck test asserting the recorded\n      `metrics[\"n_gold\"] >= 20` for every class slot.\n- [ ] The recorded results file is machine-checkable: `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/provider_parity_results.json`\n      carries, per class slot, `passed`, `gating_failures`, the full `metrics` block, both arms' resolved model\n      ids, the corpus digest and an epoch count `>= 3`; verified by the offline recheck test, which fails if\n      any of those keys is missing.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] Step 1's invokability probe result for account 896586841071 / us-east-1 is recorded\n      as a comment on this ticket: for each candidate inference-profile id, invokable or the exact API error\n      string. Evidence: the pasted `aws bedrock-runtime converse` outcome per id.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] The live paired run's `ParityReport` is attached as a comment on this ticket, stating\n      pass or fail against EACH `parity.py` gating criterion by name — validity, verdict agreement, zero\n      decision flips, recall, false-accept, runtime error rate, AND the gold-coverage guard — with the measured\n      numbers, plus the informational per-arm latency.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] The v2 arm is proven provider-clean: the same comment records the `REBAR_USAGE_LOG`\n      per-call model tally showing zero non-Bedrock calls on the v2 arm.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] If any criterion fails, a comment on this ticket records the decision taken —\n      remediate, or drop Bedrock's first-class claim.\n- [ ] [operator-attested] The decision is carried to epic 061c-ecd1-8967-4a76 as a COMMENT, not a description\n      edit. Tagged because a ticket field is not a repository file and the completion verifier reads repository\n      files only. SEQUENCING HAZARD, and the reason this is spelled out rather than merely tagged: editing the\n      epic's DESCRIPTION is a material edit that invalidates its plan-review attestation, and the epic closes\n      last, after every child — so an edit made when this criterion naturally lands would force a fresh review\n      of a very large plan at the worst moment. A comment is not material and carries the same information. If\n      a description edit is later judged necessary anyway, make it BEFORE the epic's final review."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785697619285608001,"uuid":"8ed35596-17b2-41af-a212-86e4a12962c2"}